  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 sm:px-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">How HEIC Conversion Works</h2>
    <p class="mb-4">
      Apple HEIC (High Efficiency Image Container) files use the HEIF standard for image storage.
      This format provides excellent compression but has limited browser support. Our tool uses
      <strong>libheif compiled to WebAssembly</strong> to decode HEIC files entirely in your browser.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-4">
      Once decoded, images are drawn to an <strong>off-screen canvas</strong> and exported to your
      chosen format (JPEG, PNG, or WebP) at the selected quality level. For batch processing, output files
      are bundled into a .zip archive using jszip.
    </p>
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3">Supported Formats</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside mb-6 space-y-2">
      <li>Input: HEIC, HEIF (Apple Photos format)</li>
      <li>Output: JPEG, PNG, WebP (user-selected)</li>
      <li>Quality control: Adjustable compression levels</li>
      <li>Batch processing: Drag multiple files simultaneously</li>
      <li>Export: .zip archive with all converted files</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <div class="border-b pb-4">
        <h4 class="font-medium mb-2">Is my image data safe?</h4>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">
          Yes. All decoding and conversion happens in your browser using WebAssembly. No images are
          uploaded to any server. Your files remain completely private.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-b pb-4">
        <h4 class="font-medium mb-2">Does this work for videos?</h4>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">
          No. This tool is specifically for HEIC/HEIF image files. Video files use different codecs
          and are not supported by this transcoder.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-b pb-4">
        <h4 class="font-medium mb-2">Can it work offline?</h4>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">
          Yes! Once the WebAssembly module is loaded, all conversion happens locally. The PWA service
          worker caches the library for offline use.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
